Source package: mutter

tap-key.patch
    Lets modifiers tapped on their own send key combinations, configured with tap-keys in
    org.gnome.mutter, a map from a modifier to the key combination it sends. Mutter already gives a
    lone tap of a modifier a meaning for two keys, the overlay key and the locate-pointer key, and
    this adds more. Held together with other keys, a tap key stays an ordinary modifier.

    The combination goes through a virtual keyboard, so keybinding processing handles it and it
    reaches keybindings and global shortcuts. configure-gnome-keys.sh sets tap-keys: the right Super
    sends XF86Launch9, which no physical key sends, for Claude Desktop's Quick Entry shortcut. The tap
    keys do nothing until configure-gnome-keys.sh has set tap-keys. The release of the tapped key
    still reaches the focused window, which saw it pressed.

    The test is added to src/tests/keybindings.c, which runs during the build. It configures two tap
    keys and holds each together with another key, also with the other tap key, which must send
    nothing, and then taps each on its own, which must trigger a keybinding bound to the combination
    it sends.

    The tap is detected by process_special_modifier_key in src/core/keybindings.c, shared by the
    overlay key, the locate-pointer key and the tap keys, and the settings are read in
    src/core/prefs.c. After a version bump, check that both still handle the overlay and
    locate-pointer keys this way, since the patch follows them.

    Check by hand after a rebuild and a new login:

      tap the right Super on its own     should send XF86Launch9, which opens Claude Quick Entry
      right Super with another key       should act as Super, for example Ctrl+Super+L locks

xdg-session-management.patch
    Backport of https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/mutter/-/merge_requests/4806 from mutter 51. Mutter
    50 has the session management protocol only under its draft name xx_session_manager_v1, and
    offers it only when gnome-shell runs with MUTTER_DEBUG_SESSION_MANAGEMENT_PROTOCOL=1. The patch
    renames it to the final xdg_session_manager_v1 and offers it to every client. A client which
    names its windows then has mutter restore their workspace, monitor, position, size and state.

    It is for Firefox, which binds only xdg_session_manager_v1 since version 154. Firefox restores
    its windows with it only together with the patches in patches/firefox/, which fix Mozilla bug
    2064100 and enable the protocol on GNOME again, and the one in patches/gtk+3.0/, which adds the
    xdg-toplevel-realized signal Firefox needs. Without them, MOZ_LOG=WidgetWayland:4 shows which
    check in IsSessionRestoreSupported (Firefox's widget/gtk/nsAppShell.cpp) disabled it.

    Firefox sends get_session at every start, even where it does not register its windows. Mutter
    50.1 and 51.1 crash when a client sends get_session after the session manager could not be
    created, which happens when ~/.local/share/gnome-shell/session.gvdb cannot be read (mutter
    issue 5168, open). This patch makes that crash reachable. If gnome-shell ever crashes when
    Firefox starts, look in the journal for "Could not create session manager" and move that file
    away.

    session.gvdb only grows, by about 270 bytes for every window Firefox ever had. Firefox never
    removes a window from its session, and mutter never prunes stored windows on its own. Mutter
    also cannot remove a window stored in an earlier run of a session, so a client could not clean
    up even if it tried (mutter issue 5174, open, with patches). Moving the file away resets all
    stored window places, of every application.

    The patch is the four commits of the merge request. Only the test change of the last one
    differs: upstream it edits src/tests/wayland-xdg-session-management-tests.c, which was split
    out of src/tests/wayland-unit-tests.c after 50.1, so here the same line is removed from
    wayland-unit-tests.c. The existing session management tests in wayland-unit-tests.c run during
    the build against the renamed protocol.

    Drop the patch once Ubuntu ships mutter 51 or newer. Before that, after a version bump, check
    that src/meson.build still has no xdg-session-management entry of its own, and note that with
    wayland-protocols 1.48 or newer the build uses the protocol XML from wayland-protocols instead of
    the copy in src/wayland/protocol/.

    Check by hand after a rebuild and a new login, with wayland-info from the wayland-utils package:

      wayland-info | grep session        should list xdg_session_manager_v1, and xx_session_manager_v1
                                         from xx-session-management.patch

xx-session-management.patch
    Offers the draft protocol, xx_session_manager_v1, again next to the final one, for Chromium.
    Chromium speaks only the draft (up to at least 156, see crbug.com/409099413), so with
    xdg-session-management.patch alone it found no session manager. Both protocols are served by the
    same code and share the stored sessions, and each session records which one it was created
    through. For the draft, add_toplevel refuses only a name in use by a toplevel of the same
    session object, as in mutter 50.1, which Chromium was written against. Mutter 50.1 offered the
    draft only with MUTTER_DEBUG_SESSION_MANAGEMENT_PROTOCOL=1, this offers it to every client.

    Chromium uses it only with its WaylandSessionManagement feature, enabled in
    chrome://flags/#wayland-session-management or with --enable-features=WaylandSessionManagement,
    which chromium.d/ of this repository adds. Without the feature, Chromium and the Electron
    applications built on it bind the global but send nothing, checked with Chromium, Signal and
    Slack. Chromium names each window anew at every start: it restores a window under its previous
    name, then removes that name and adds the window under a new one, so unlike Firefox it does not
    make session.gvdb grow. As with Firefox, the first start after enabling it shows windows
    unplaced, and from the next start on they return to their places.

    The session management tests in wayland-unit-tests.c run during the build against the final
    protocol, which the patch leaves as it is. The draft is tested with Chromium itself by
    tests/mutter/run-chromium-session.sh, see tests/mutter/README.

    Mutter 51 drops the draft, so when Ubuntu ships it, this patch has to be ported to mutter 51's
    own code. Drop it once Chromium speaks xdg_session_manager_v1.

xdg-toplevel-session-rename.patch
    Implements the rename request of xdg_toplevel_session_v1, which mutter leaves unimplemented,
    also in 51.1 and on main. libwayland-server aborts a compositor which receives a request without
    a handler, so any client sending rename ended GNOME Shell and with it the session.
    xdg-session-management.patch offers the protocol to every client, so the two go together. A name
    already known to the session raises name_in_use, as add_toplevel does, and otherwise the state
    stored under the old name moves to the new one.

    Neither Firefox nor Chromium sends rename. tests/mutter/run-rename-check.sh checks it with a
    minimal client, see tests/mutter/README. The crash is reported as mutter issue 5177. Drop the
    patch once mutter implements rename.

save-toplevels-on-session-destroy.patch
    Mutter saves where a window of a session was only when the window is unmanaged, and only while
    the session object still exists, while the protocol says destroying a session preserves the
    current state of its toplevels. When a client quits without destroying its windows first,
    libwayland destroys its objects in the order of their IDs, so a window whose surface, toplevel
    and xdg_wm_base all have higher IDs than the session is unmanaged only once the session is gone,
    and its place is lost. GTK 4 binds xdg_wm_base and creates every window but the first after its
    session, and quits without destroying its windows, so Text Editor, quitting with several windows
    open, had only its first window placed again at the next start. With this patch mutter saves the
    state of every toplevel still having a window when the session is destroyed, by the client or by
    its disconnect, unless the client removed the session. The same code serves
    xdg_session_manager_v1 and the draft from xx-session-management.patch.

    The test xdg-session-management-restore of src/tests/wayland-unit-tests.c is run a second time
    with the client destroying its session while its toplevel is still mapped
    (restore-session-destroyed), which fails without the change. The tests run during the build.
    The fix is proposed upstream as mutter merge request 5453. Drop the patch once mutter saves a
    session's toplevels when the session is destroyed.

save-toplevels-on-change.patch
    Mutter stores where a window of a session is only when the window is unmanaged, and with the
    patch above when the session is destroyed, while the protocol says the compositor updates the
    state of a toplevel whenever it changes. When GNOME Shell crashes, or the machine resets or
    loses power, no window is unmanaged, so every window comes back where it was the last time it
    was stored, possibly days earlier, or opens unplaced. With this patch mutter stores the state
    of a toplevel whenever its window moves, changes its size, its maximized, fullscreen or
    minimized state, or its workspace, and writes session.gvdb 3 seconds after the first change,
    as it did for a window unmanaged. Windows without a workspace, not placed yet or shown on all
    workspaces, are left to the store at unmanage, as is a toplevel the client removed. A restored
    toplevel is stored on change only once its stored state has been applied, which would
    otherwise be replaced while it is read.

    The test restore-changed of src/tests/wayland-unit-tests.c has a second client restore the
    session while the first one still has its moved window mapped, and fails without the change,
    with the window placed anew. The tests run during the build. With Ptyxis it is also tested by
    tests/ptyxis/run-ptyxis-session.sh, which ends mutter with SIGKILL and starts it
    again, and whose second run restores no window without this patch. Reported upstream as
    mutter issue 5181 and proposed as mutter merge request 5455. Drop the patch once mutter stores
    a toplevel's state when it changes.

wayland-keyboard-test-sync-point.patch
    Upstream's commit c6a16523f, which fixes a race in the keyboard tests of the build's test suite.
    A test waited for the test client's window and then for its sync point, and waiting for the
    window could dispatch the sync point request, so the test hung until meson killed it after 360
    seconds and failed the build. It changes only the test. Drop the patch once Ubuntu ships mutter
    51, which has it.

hidden-window-updates.patch
    Mutter freezes the updates of a Wayland window until the window is shown for the first time,
    and until then the window's actor stays 0x0, without the client's content. A window restored to
    another workspace is shown only when that workspace is activated, so after logging in, the
    overview's workspace thumbnails showed nothing for the restored windows of workspaces not visited
    yet. With this patch the updates of a hidden window which is placed or not floating are no longer
    frozen: such a window already has its monitor and size. A floating window has them from its
    stored state or because mutter places a hidden window as soon as it has a buffer, and a
    maximized, tiled or fullscreen one from the constraints on its monitor, which for a restored
    window is the monitor of its stored state. Windows shown when they map are unchanged.

    The tests restore-other-workspace, restore-maximized-other-workspace (also with a client which
    requests being maximized itself, as GTK does) and restore-tiled-other-workspace of
    src/tests/wayland-unit-tests.c restore a window, floating, maximized or tiled on a second
    monitor, to the second workspace while the first one is active, and fail without the change, as
    the window's actor is 0x0. They then activate the second workspace and check that the window is
    shown unchanged, with its actor following it. The tests run during the build. The session tests
    overview-thumbnails and restore-monitors in tests/session/ check it in GNOME Shell's overview
    with Ptyxis. The same symptom for windows which appear while the overview is open is mutter issue
    1752. Not proposed upstream yet.

keymap-state-before-key-events.patch
    Mutter's input thread queues the keymap state resulting from a key event before the event, but
    the main thread can still handle the event first, since GLib releases its lock while it checks
    a source, and then mutter sends no wl_keyboard.modifiers for a modifier key. The client keeps
    its previous modifiers until the next key or button event, so scrolling and pointer motion
    carry them, as with Alt in mutter issue 4315. With this patch the backend's event source waits
    until the queued keymap state is applied.

    Without it, the build's test wayland-keyboard, whose client waits for that event after each
    modifier key, hangs in about 2.5% of its runs under load until meson kills it after 360
    seconds. Reported as mutter issue 5216, with the fix not proposed upstream yet.

restore-target-monitor.patch
    Upstream's change from mutter 50.2, which gives a window a target monitor, the monitor window
    management operations act on. Restoring a maximized, tiled or fullscreen window sets it from the
    stored rectangle, and the constraints fall back to it, where 50.1 moved the window to the stored
    rectangle first and fell back to the monitor the window was created on. The patch also has the
    fix from mutter 50.3 for a crash after a display reconfiguration, which the target monitor
    introduced (mutter issue 4891). restore-maximized-client-monitor.patch builds on it. Drop the
    patch once Ubuntu ships mutter 50.3 or newer.

restore-maximized-client-monitor.patch
    GTK requests being maximized before it restores a window it restores maximized, so mutter has
    maximized the window on the monitor it was created on by the time the restore sets its target
    monitor, and maximizing it again leaves it there: a Ptyxis window saved maximized on a second
    monitor returned maximized on the first one, also with restore-target-monitor.patch. With this
    patch restoring moves a window which already is maximized or fullscreen to the monitor of its
    stored state. The test restore-maximized-other-workspace-client-maximized of
    src/tests/wayland-unit-tests.c, whose client requests being maximized before restoring, as GTK
    does, fails without it, and the session test restore-monitors in tests/session/ checks it with
    Ptyxis on two monitors. Not checked against upstream's main, not proposed upstream yet.

wayland-client-exited-subprocess.patch
    Mutter's function for starting a program as a Wayland client, which DING uses for its desktop
    icons process, crashed GNOME Shell with SIGSEGV when the program was killed right after it
    started: the function reads the program's pid from GLib, which no longer knows it once its
    worker thread has reaped the program. At a logout systemd kills every process of GNOME Shell's
    unit, so a DING process started at that moment, as DING does when it is enabled again after
    unlocking, crashed GNOME Shell in a CI run of the session test lock-screen. With this patch
    such a client is left without a pid. With a library preloaded into GNOME Shell which delays
    GLib's answer, so that GLib has reaped a program which exits at once, GNOME Shell crashed at the
    first such start without the patch, and survived 20 of 20 with it. Ubuntu's Error Tracker has
    the same crash from gnome-shell 49 (Launchpad bug 2126624). Upstream's main has the same code,
    the fix is not proposed upstream yet.

keep-keyboard-focus.patch
    Adds meta_display_set_grab_keeps_keyboard_focus(), which marks an actor whose stage grabs leave
    the Wayland keyboard focus of the focused window in place. GNOME Shell grabs the stage for every
    menu it opens, which moves the keyboard focus away from the focused window, and Chromium and
    Electron derive the active state of a window from its keyboard focus. A menu of the global menu
    extension (gnome-extensions/global-menu@mitar.tnode.com) opened for such a window would make the
    window inactive, and VS Code drops the action of a menu item when it finds no focused window. The
    extension marks its menus.

    During a marked grab, key events still go only to the grab. Repeating keys the window received as
    pressed are released for it when the grab starts. When the grab ends, the keys the window received as
    pressed and which were released meanwhile are released for it, and the modifier state is sent again,
    so that Alt of Alt+F, which opens a menu, does not stay pressed for the window. The input method focus
    leaves the window for the time of the grab.
    Xwayland keeps the usual behavior. ClutterStage gains a grab-actor property, notified whenever the actor of its
    topmost grab changes, and the keyboard focus is decided again then, which a move from a global
    menu to the date menu needs.

    The test wayland-keep-keyboard-focus, which runs during the build, covers a marked grab with a
    held key and a held modifier, an unmarked grab, and a grab which moves from a marked actor to an
    unmarked one and back. It fails when any of the parts is removed: releasing the held keys at the start
    or at the end of the grab, sending the modifiers again, or deciding again when the grab actor changes.
    Mutter's tests have no input method, so the session test global-menu-input-method (tests/session/)
    covers moving the focus of the input method away: with IBus composing Chinese text in the window, the
    letter of an item and Escape reach an open global menu, and without that part the input method
    receives both. The session test global-menu-date-menu checks deciding again in GNOME Shell: the
    pointer moved from an open global menu onto the clock opens the date menu, and the window loses its
    keyboard focus only with that part.

    Not proposed upstream yet. After a version bump, check meta_wayland_event_handler_invalidate_focus()
    in src/wayland/meta-wayland-input.c, the one place which decides the keyboard focus during a
    grab, and clutter_stage_notify_grab() in clutter/clutter/clutter-stage.c.

    Check by hand after a rebuild and a new login, with VS Code focused:

      open a menu in the top bar         VS Code's window should stay active
      choose View > Toggle Full Screen   should make VS Code's window fullscreen

kde-appmenu.patch
    Offers KDE's org_kde_kwin_appmenu protocol, with which Chromium, Firefox and Qt name the D-Bus
    address of the com.canonical.dbusmenu menu of a Wayland window, and gives the address to GNOME
    Shell as the MetaWindow properties dbus-appmenu-service-name and dbus-appmenu-object-path, from
    which the global menu extension reads the menu of the focused window. The protocol XML is a copy
    from plasma-wayland-protocols. Electron does not use the protocol for its menus (electron issue
    34335, pull request 54629 open), so the extension finds them through com.canonical.AppMenu.Registrar.

    The test wayland-kde-appmenu, which runs during the build, has a client name, rename and release
    an address, and checks the properties and their notifications after each step.

    Local only: wayland-protocols merge request 493 (dbus_annotation) is the protocol GNOME has
    agreed to, so far for accessibility. Once mutter implements it and the browsers name their
    menus with it, this patch can go. After a version bump, check that mutter still has no
    org_kde_kwin_appmenu of its own.

    Check by hand after a rebuild and a new login, with wayland-info from the wayland-utils package:

      wayland-info | grep appmenu        should list org_kde_kwin_appmenu_manager

shell-shows-menubar.patch
    Adds meta_display_set_shell_shows_menubar(), with which GNOME Shell announces the gtk_shell1
    capability GLOBAL_MENU_BAR, and GTK hides the menu bar of a GtkApplicationWindow while the
    capability is announced. The global menu extension sets it while it is enabled, so GTK 3 and GTK 4
    applications show their menu bars again when the extension is disabled.

    The test wayland-gtk-shell-menubar, which runs during the build, checks the capability event a
    client receives when the switch changes, and the capability a client which binds gtk_shell1
    receives while it is set.

    Not proposed upstream yet. After a version bump, check calculate_capabilities() in
    src/wayland/meta-wayland-gtk-shell.c.

watched-modifiers.patch
    Adds meta_display_set_watched_modifiers(), which names modifier keys whose presses and releases
    the display reports with the signals watched-modifier-pressed, when the key is pressed while no
    other modifier is held, watched-modifier-combined, when another key or a pointer button is
    pressed while it is held, and watched-modifier-released, which says whether the key was tapped
    on its own. The keys still reach the focused window, and nothing is reported while the focused
    window inhibits shortcuts. GNOME Shell otherwise receives a key only when it is bound or while it
    has the keyboard. The global menu extension watches the left Alt, which with the repository's
    key rotation is the physical Win key: while it is held on its own, the titles of the menus
    underline the letters of their mnemonics, and a tap opens the menu of the application's name.

    The test /core/keybindings/watched-modifiers is added to src/tests/keybindings.c, which runs
    during the build. It checks the signals for a tap, for the key held together with another key,
    for a press while another modifier is held and for the other Alt key, which is not watched, and
    that nothing is reported once no key is watched.

    Not proposed upstream yet. The reports come from process_key_event() and
    meta_keybindings_process_event() in src/core/keybindings.c, next to the handling of the overlay
    key, the locate-pointer key and tap-key.patch. After a version bump, check that every key event
    still passes there.

    Check by hand after a rebuild and a new login, with a window which has menus focused:

      hold the physical Win key          should underline the letters of the menu titles
      tap it                             should open the menu of the application's name
      Win+Tab                            should switch windows, with the underlines gone

forward-key-event.patch
    Adds meta_display_forward_key_event(), which delivers a key event, which a stage grab received, to
    the client with the keyboard focus, as it is delivered without a grab, once the stage is not grabbed
    anymore. A shortcut of the focused window pressed while a menu of the global menu extension is open
    reaches the menu's grab, and the extension closes the menu and forwards the key, so the shortcut acts
    as if the menu were not open. With keep-keyboard-focus.patch, the window kept its keyboard focus
    during the grab and receives the modifier state when the grab ends, so the key reaches it with the
    modifiers held, and the release of the key follows without a grab.

    The test /wayland/keep-keyboard-focus/forward-key is added to wayland-keep-keyboard-focus, which
    runs during the build. It checks that Ctrl+N pressed during a grab is not forwarded while the stage
    is grabbed, and that the client receives N with Ctrl held once the grab ended, followed by the
    releases of N and Ctrl.

    Not proposed upstream yet, as it builds on keep-keyboard-focus.patch. After a version bump, check
    that meta_wayland_compositor_handle_event() still delivers a key event to the client with the
    keyboard focus.

    Check by hand after a rebuild and a new login, with VS Code focused:

      open a menu in the top bar         then Ctrl+Shift+N should close it and open a new window

global-menu-symbols.patch (debian-series)
    Lists the functions keep-keyboard-focus.patch, kde-appmenu.patch, shell-shows-menubar.patch,
    watched-modifiers.patch and forward-key-event.patch add in debian/libmutter-18-0.symbols. Ubuntu
    builds with DPKG_GENSYMBOLS_CHECK_LEVEL=4, which fails the build on any exported symbol the file
    does not list. It changes the packaging, so it is applied from debian-series rather than series.

keep-keyboard-focus.patch, kde-appmenu.patch and shell-shows-menubar.patch apply in this order, since
each edits lines next to the ones the previous one adds.
